  "prompt_text": "The figure maps a radioactive decay and neutron-capture network among isotopes of mercury, thallium, lead, bismuth, and polonium. Colored circles represent individual nuclides, with a small legend indicating that the color scale reflects relative stability: redder nodes are more stable, while yellow nodes are less stable. Curved connecting lines distinguish the transformation type, including neutron capture, beta decay, electron capture, and alpha decay.\n\nOverall, the diagram shows how isotopes in this mass region are linked by competing pathways. A horizontal chain of lead isotopes is especially prominent, connected by neutron capture steps, while nearby mercury and thallium isotopes feed into or away from this chain through beta decay and electron capture. On the right, bismuth and polonium isotopes extend the network upward, with a notable long-lived polonium isotope and an alpha-decay arrow returning material toward lead. Several half-lives are written beside selected isotopes, ranging from minutes and hours to years and even extremely long timescales, emphasizing strong differences in persistence across the network.\n\nTaken together, the image summarizes nucleosynthesis and decay flow in a heavy-element isotopic region, highlighting both stable stopping points and short-lived branching intermediates.",
